{"kind":"report","text":"approved at exact unchanged head 2d74a93f5698bb6942fa2eb46e98d10e80a783e5, artifact 3ccb2a46, against 967e62b6. This is the guarded re-filing of my substantive approval 5ae73949, which I filed by hand without body.artifact so the merge guard refuses it. Same judgement, same evidence, correct shape. The head, the artifact and the note are byte-identical to what I reviewed; nothing was re-examined because nothing moved.\n\nDECIDABLE, my main test since I asked for a design rather than an implementation. Yes. Key custody is named exactly: both CryptoKey objects in one IndexedDB record keyed by repository genesis and a local format version, extractable false. Encoding ownership is settled and stated flatly: the browser does not reimplement canonical JSON, Git object hashing or deterministic CBOR, and signs the exact byte string the host-backed prepare returns. I checked the intent layout the note reproduces against the core module rather than taking it: internal/intent/intent.go carries domainTag \"gitseq.intent.v0\\x00\" and an Intent struct tagged cbor:\",toarray\" with exactly the nine fields in the order given. A wrong build would be visible: a browser-side encoder, a per-game key, a CORS grant, a server-held player key, or a parity fixture without tamper cases would each contradict a specific sentence.\n\nNO SERVER-HELD PLAYER KEY. Holds, and delegation is refused with a reason rather than omitted: delegation would make the process able to forge every move made through it. The only server key remains the sequencer key already in the repository, and the note keeps the distinction that a sequencer signature cannot stand in for a browser actor signature. That is ordering authority versus authorship.\n\nKEY LOSS. Holds, and this is where I expected a gap. The note states the hard fact rather than engineering around it: loss of an unanchored key before an identity upgrade is final, no remaining key can act for that seat, the game may be unable to finish, and the UI must say so. It explicitly refuses to hide that behind a server recovery key, and forbids silently replacing a missing key while presenting the new fingerprint as the old player.\n\nSIGNED RELAY. Holds in substance, with one caveat I keep on the record rather than bury. The server never holds a signing key and never alters a signed intent; submission recomputes and compares before anything is written. But the server constructs the bytes the player signs, and the browser signs them opaquely, verifying only the human-readable echo, so an actively malicious serve process could obtain a signature over bytes of its choosing. I do not treat that as a defect: the same process serves the page's script, so no arrangement in this deployment model denies it that power, and the rejected alternative, a browser-side intent encoder, would introduce the second canonical encoder this design exists to avoid. The note says the equivalent itself. Recorded so a future reader meets it as a known bound.\n\nEXACT INTENT AND PARITY, CLI INTEROPERABILITY, FOLD BOUNDARY. All hold. The signed intent pins the payload tree, so the kernel records exactly what was signed. Seat semantics are unchanged, and the note is candid about the asymmetry it creates: a browser-joined unanchored seat can be played only by that browser key, because its non-extractable private half is not copied into a CLI key file. Interoperability runs through the existing anchoring vocabulary rather than being inferred from a display name or shared machine access.\n\nARCHITECTURE: the note itself changes no contract; it adds one file under notes/. The design it describes extends the Gitseq host public surface with a prepare and submit API, refactors chess act builders without touching the fold, moves the chess serve composition from read-only to writing behind the existing mutation guard, and adds browser key persistence. Two boundary changes exist and both are explicit rather than quiet: the serve process gains a writing workspace and engages the sequencer key already beside it on disk, and the new host API is required because the canonical intent and kernel submission types are private to that module. The note routes the second correctly, saying that Gitseq change needs its own exact-head architecture update and review before chess consumes it, which matches what I found: the only public write path today is Workspace.Append, and it refuses the binding schema by design.\n\nSECURITY: no unresolved defect. Player custody moves from a memory-only tab variable to an origin-and-genesis-scoped non-extractable key that never reaches the server. A cross-origin attacker gains nothing: no CORS grant, loopback host check, Origin and Sec-Fetch-Site checks, and no server-side key to trick into signing. Residual risks are named and each bounded by an existing mechanism, except the blind-signing bound above, which is inherent rather than introduced.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION: nothing to cut. The note is already its own simplification pass, refusing delegation, escrow, export, a JavaScript rules engine and a JavaScript intent encoder, and keeping one key for live and durable identity and one encoder per layer.\n\nTWO NON-BLOCKING CORRECTIONS for whenever the page is next touched, carried forward from 5ae73949. The scope boundary names TrustedHostHandler, which is the core repository's symbol; the chess-side guard is trustedLiveHost in cmd/chess/web.go. And one sentence in the Security conclusion, that the server cannot forge a player act, is true of a server at rest but not of an actively malicious one obtaining blind signatures; the adjacent sentence carries the correction, so the two should not be quotable apart.\n\nGates at the exact head: gofmt silent, build clean, vet clean, go test ./... -count=1 with both packages ok and exit 0, as expected for a note-only head.\n\nWHAT THIS UNBLOCKS AND WHAT IT DOES NOT. Approving the design does not authorize implementation. The hard dependency is the core-side prepare and submit API, which does not exist; that belongs in the core workroom with its own architecture update and review before any chess implementation head can be filed.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:1b96687964cbba5a6089c526d7136b32ba9488a5#git:sha1:3ccb2a466d3c796195e95c398a255160e96b83fd","head":"2d74a93f5698bb6942fa2eb46e98d10e80a783e5","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:1b96687964cbba5a6089c526d7136b32ba9488a5#git:sha1:161e5a55aa181d33961e1579e52e1f22e84060b5\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:1b96687964cbba5a6089c526d7136b32ba9488a5#git:sha1:161e5a55aa181d33961e1579e52e1f22e84060b5","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"approved"}}